  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose a novel approach combining multi-scale optimization with quadratic programming to find improved step functions for minimizing C\u2085.\n\nKey innovations:\n- Coarse-to-fine optimization: Start with a low-resolution grid to escape local minima, then refine solution at high resolution\n- Quadratic programming formulation to handle non-convexity through convex relaxations\n- Gradient-based early stopping to prevent overfitting\n- Automatic learning rate adaptation based on validation\n\nThe approach addresses the primary limitation of the current method - reliance on gradient descent prone to local optima - while maintaining computational efficiency. Key assumptions: multi-scale initialization helps escape local minima, and convex relaxations provide useful bounds.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe model h \u2208 [0,1]^{n_points} as a step function with sum(h) = n_points/2. The objective function is:\n\nC\u2085 = max_k (h * (1 - h_k)) \u22c5 dx, where h_k is h shifted by k steps\n\nExact representation:\n- Discretize domain [0,2] into n_points intervals\n- Use zero-padding for shifts beyond domain boundaries\n- Formulate as min-max problem with quadratic constraints\n\nNew elements:\n- Coarse-to-fine optimization path\n- Quadratic penalty for overlap constraints\n- Progressive refinement of solution\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Initialization:\n1. Use initial_h_values if provided\n2. Start with coarse grid (n_start=50)\n3. If initial_h available, downsample using linear interpolation\n4. Initialize fine grid solver with coarse solution\n\nb) Coarse grid optimization:\n1. Formulate quadratic program with overlapping constraints\n2. Solve with CVXPY using ECOS solver\n3. Track best solution and validation metrics\n4. Apply early stopping based on validation performance\n\nc) Transfer to fine grid:\n1. Upsample optimal coarse solution\n2. Initialize fine grid solver with upscaled coarse solution\n3. Use coarse solution as starting point\n\nd) Fine grid optimization:\n1. Solve refined quadratic program\n2. Apply adaptive learning rate\n3. Project to feasible region after each step\n4. Track best solution and validate\n\ne) Constraint handling:\n1. Clip h to [0,1] after each update\n2. Normalize sum(h) = n_points / 2 exactly\n3. Use quadratic penalties for constraint violations\n\nf) Termination:\n1. Stop if time budget exhausted\n2. Early stopping if no improvement for 300 steps\n3. Return best solution with valid objective value\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Initial grid size: 50 (coarse), final grid size: 200\n- Solver parameters: max_iter=1000, tol=1e-6\n- Learning rate: adaptive with min=1e-5, max=1e-3\n- Early stopping patience: 300 iterations\n- Quadratic penalty weight: 1e-4\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Coarse optimization: ~50s\n- Fine optimization: ~250s\n- Total: ~300s\n- Robustness: Early stopping, feasible fallback\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Final solution validated via evaluate_erdos_solution\n- Check constraints: sum(h) = n_points / 2 with tolerance 1e-6\n- Ensure all h \u2208 [0,1]\n- Return in required format (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)",
